        "image_alt": "A-DIY composite deck beside a stone patio with lounge seating"
      }
    },
    "story": {
      "type": "dvp-story",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "It starts at the back door",
        "heading": "It starts with one small moment: coffee in hand, back door open, stepping out into your own backyard.",
        "body": "For a lot of homes, that moment never feels quite right. The door sits a step too high above the lawn. The yard tilts toward the fence. The grass stays soft for days after rain. So the chairs stay folded, and the backyard waits for a better day.",
        "payoff": "A patio has to be dug into that ground to fix it. A deck simply rises above it, meeting your door so your first step lands on a level, dry floor. It’s there for the slow morning coffee, the long summer dinner and the evening you didn’t plan to stay out so late, season after season.",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/dvp-story.png",
        "image_alt": "A-DIY wood deck with steps that meets the back door"
      }
    },
    "answer": {
      "type": "dvp-answer",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Deck or patio: which is better for your backyard?",
        "lead": "For most backyards, a deck is the more reliable long-term choice.",
        "body": "Choose a deck if your yard slopes, your door sits above the ground, the soil holds water, or you want upkeep you can plan for. Choose a patio if your yard is flat, firm and drains well, and you want a hard surface at ground level. Like the low look of a patio? A ground-level deck gives you both."
      },
